    {
      "actual_test_execution": false,
      "finding": "Exact dict type does not constrain key types. set(input) is built and compared before exact-str key and cardinality validation. A custom key with stored hash matching an expected Source name may run its custom equality during scope set comparison and subsequent input[name] lookup, before any source byte digest check. This is a symbolic Python data-model consequence; no callback was executed. There is also no input item-count bound before set allocation. The declared rejection of custom objects/callbacks is therefore not implemented at these public byte-loader boundaries. This is not an assertion that Python is a sandbox.",
      "id": "C46-R1-PUBLIC-BYTE-LOADER-CUSTOM-KEY-CALLBACK-AND-PRECHECK-BOUND",
      "locations": [
        "Source/memory_entry.py:24",
        "Source/memory_entry.py:27",
        "Source/extractor.py:42",
        "Source/extractor.py:45"
      ],
      "negative_specification_only": [
        "custom key whose hash/equality impersonates one expected name must refuse before any callback",
        "str subclass key must refuse before any equality callback",
        "wrong dict cardinality must refuse before copying a potentially large key set"
      ],
      "required_change": "At both public byte-source boundaries check exact dict, exact expected len, and exact builtin-str key types by bounded key iteration before set comparisons or name lookups. Then compare expected names and require exact bytes values/pins. Alternatively supply an exact reviewed existing Root reader enforcing this grammar before any Source invocation; no such runner is bound in this packet. Add bounded custom-key and over-cardinality negative controls; do not run the current public entry.",
